var e={id:`bundle-design`,h:{en:`Bundles in the core: BFD over bundles, metrics, ECMP and multichassis designs`,hi:`Core mein bundles: bundles par BFD, metrics, ECMP aur multichassis designs`},body:{en:`
<p>A bundle is like a team of delivery vans on the same route. The dispatcher (the IGP) sees one team, but you must still check that every van is really driving, that the dispatcher knows the team has shrunk when two vans break down, and that you do not mix the team with separate vans in confusing ways. This chapter joins the ideas of the last chapters: BFD with bundles, metrics, ECMP versus LAG, and multichassis link aggregation.</p>
<div class="dia"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 145" role="img" aria-label="Two BFD options for a bundle: a session per member link, or one session on the logical interface."><rect x="20" y="30" width="133" height="70" rx="10" fill="#bae6fd"/><text x="86.5" y="58" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-ink" font-size="13">Member 1</text><text x="86.5" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-muted" font-size="12">BFD per member</text><line x1="153" y1="65" x2="175" y2="65" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="175" y="30" width="133" height="70" rx="10" fill="#bbf7d0"/><text x="241.5" y="58" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-ink" font-size="13">Member 2</text><text x="241.5" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-muted" font-size="12">BFD per member</text><line x1="308" y1="65" x2="330" y2="65" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="330" y="30" width="133" height="70" rx="10" fill="#ddd6fe"/><text x="396.5" y="58" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-ink" font-size="13">Bundle</text><text x="396.5" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-muted" font-size="12">BFD on logical IF</text><line x1="463" y1="65" x2="485" y2="65" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="485" y="30" width="133" height="70" rx="10" fill="#fde68a"/><text x="551.5" y="58" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-ink" font-size="13">IGP</text><text x="551.5" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-muted" font-size="12">one adjacency</text><text x="320" y="128" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-accent" font-size="12">Per-member BFD finds a bad member; logical BFD finds a bad bundle path</text></svg><p class="cap">Two BFD options for a bundle: a session per member link, or one session on the logical interface.</p></div>
<h3>BFD over a bundle</h3>
<p>If you run BFD on a Layer 3 bundle interface, the session uses one member link at a time as its path, chosen by hashing. A fault on a different member may not be noticed, and traffic hashed onto that member is lost until LACP or the physical layer removes it. Cisco IOS XR therefore offers two models:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>BFD over Bundle (BoB).</b> An IETF-standard approach (RFC 7130, micro-BFD): one BFD session <b>per member link</b>. If a member's session fails, that member is removed from the bundle and the bundle stays up with the remaining members. This finds a bad member quickly and also detects a member that is up at the physical layer but not forwarding.</li>
<li><b>BFD over Logical Bundle (BLB).</b> A single BFD session on the bundle interface, treated as one Layer 3 link. If the session goes down, the whole bundle is declared down. Simple, but only one hashed path is verified at a time.</li>
</ul>
<pre class="code"><span class="c">! Reference configuration (not run in the simulator)</span>
<span class="c">! IOS XR: per-member BFD (BoB)</span>
interface Bundle-Ether1
 bfd mode ietf
 bfd address-family ipv4 destination 198.51.100.2
 bfd address-family ipv4 fast-detect
 bfd address-family ipv4 minimum-interval 50
 bfd address-family ipv4 multiplier 3
 ipv4 address 198.51.100.1 255.255.255.252
 bundle minimum-links 2</pre>
<p>The exact options depend on the platform and release (for example the mode value and whether echo is used), so treat the block as a model of the structure and check the command reference of your image. The exam expects you to know the two models and the reasons to pick each.</p>
<h3>Bundles and the IGP metric</h3>
<p>IS-IS and OSPF do not automatically compute the cost of a bundle from its current bandwidth on every platform. IS-IS uses the configured metric, and the default is 10 on all interfaces. OSPF derives cost from the reference bandwidth and the interface bandwidth. If a four-member 40G bundle loses two members, its capacity halves, but the metric stays as before and traffic keeps arriving. Options are: (1) a manual metric that makes the bundle less attractive when degraded, using <b>minimum-links</b> to take it down at a set threshold; (2) bundle bandwidth based metric, for example OSPF <code>auto-cost</code> in combination with bundle bandwidth on platforms that update it; (3) TE-based placement. Always decide which approach your design uses and test the failure.</p>
<h3>ECMP versus LAG</h3>
<table class="cmp"><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>LAG bundle</th><th>ECMP over separate L3 links</th></tr><tr><td>Adjacencies</td><td>One IGP adjacency and one LDP session</td><td>One per link</td></tr><tr><td>Failure of a member</td><td>Capacity drops, adjacency stays</td><td>IGP converges, ECMP set shrinks</td></tr><tr><td>Config effort</td><td>Bundle plus members</td><td>Addresses on every link</td></tr><tr><td>Load balancing</td><td>Per-flow hash on members (L2/L3/L4)</td><td>Per-flow hash on the next hop set</td></tr><tr><td>Scale</td><td>Fewer routes and adjacencies</td><td>More state, finer failure handling</td></tr><tr><td>Typical SP use</td><td>Core links between P routers, 100G aggregates</td><td>Spine-leaf style and some TE designs</td></tr></table>
<h3>Multichassis designs</h3>
<p>A customer or an access device is often connected to <b>two different PE routers</b> so a router failure does not isolate it. Two methods are important. <b>mLACP / MC-LAG</b> (multichassis LACP) lets two chassis present themselves as one LACP partner and synchronise state over an inter-chassis link (ICCP). It works but needs careful synchronisation and has operational complexity. <b>EVPN multihoming with ESI</b> (Ethernet Segment Identifier) is the modern, standards-based replacement: both PEs share an ESI and a LACP system ID derived from it, and BGP EVPN routes coordinate the designated forwarder. Pay attention that EVPN is covered in more detail in later SP modules; at this stage know the two names and that all-active multihoming spreads traffic over both PEs.</p>
<div class="eg"><p><b>Worked example.</b> Between P1 and P2 you run Bundle-Ether1 with four 100G members (400G). You set minimum-links 2, per-member BFD at 50 ms by 3 and an IS-IS metric of 10. A transponder fault silently drops one member's traffic while its optics stay up. LACP at fast rate would take 3 seconds. The BoB session on that member fails after 150 ms, the member leaves the bundle, and the hash redistributes flows over the three remaining members. The bundle stays up with 300G.</p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Common mistake.</b> Using one logical-bundle BFD session and assuming every member is verified. The session follows one hashed path. A silent fault on another member is not seen. Also avoid aggressive BFD on every member of a large bundle without checking platform scale: each session costs resources, and the count of sessions on a line card is limited.</p></div>
<div class="exam"><p><b>Exam trap.</b> BFD over Bundle (per member, RFC 7130 micro-BFD) removes a failed <i>member</i>; BFD over a Logical Bundle brings the whole bundle down. LACP detects a member failure slowly (3 seconds fast, 90 seconds slow) compared with BFD. A bundle does not change the IS-IS metric automatically on every platform.</p></div>
<div class="scn"><h4>The silent member</h4><p>A 4 x 100G bundle between two P routers showed no alarms, yet some customers saw about 25 percent packet loss. One transponder in the optical transport had failed in a way that kept the router port lit. LACP did not remove the member because the partner kept sending LACPDUs on a different internal path. After the incident the team moved from logical-bundle BFD to per-member BFD, and the next transponder fault removed the bad member in 150 ms.</p><p><b>Lesson:</b> verify every member, not just one hashed path.</p></div>
<div class="iv"><p class="q">"Compare BFD over Bundle and BFD over Logical Bundle on IOS XR."</p><p>BoB runs one BFD session per member (micro-BFD, RFC 7130). A failure removes only that member and the bundle remains up, so it catches silent member faults but uses more sessions. BLB runs one session on the bundle as a Layer 3 interface and brings the whole bundle down on failure; it is simpler but verifies one hashed path. Add that minimum-links decides when a degraded bundle must go down.</p></div>
<div class="kt"><p><b>Key takeaways</b></p><ul>
<li>BoB: BFD per member link, removes the bad member (micro-BFD, RFC 7130).</li>
<li>BLB: one BFD session on the logical bundle, brings the whole bundle down.</li>
<li>The IGP metric does not automatically follow bundle bandwidth on all platforms; use minimum-links and a deliberate metric plan.</li>
<li>ECMP gives one adjacency per link; LAG gives one adjacency per bundle.</li>
<li>MC-LAG (mLACP with ICCP) and EVPN ESI multihoming connect a device to two PEs.</li>
</ul></div>
`,hi:`
<p>Bundle ek hi route par chalne wali delivery vans ki team jaisa hai. Dispatcher (IGP) ko ek team dikhti hai, lekin aapko phir bhi check karna padta hai ki har van sach mein chal rahi hai, ki do vans kharab hone par dispatcher ko pata hai team chhoti ho gayi, aur ki aap team ko alag vans ke saath confusing tareeke se mix nahi kar rahe. Yeh chapter pichhle chapters ke ideas jodta hai: bundles ke saath BFD, metrics, ECMP versus LAG, aur multichassis link aggregation.</p>
<div class="dia"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 145" role="img" aria-label="Bundle ke liye do BFD options: har member link par ek session, ya logical interface par ek session."><rect x="20" y="30" width="133" height="70" rx="10" fill="#bae6fd"/><text x="86.5" y="58" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-ink" font-size="13">Member 1</text><text x="86.5" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-muted" font-size="12">BFD per member</text><line x1="153" y1="65" x2="175" y2="65" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="175" y="30" width="133" height="70" rx="10" fill="#bbf7d0"/><text x="241.5" y="58" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-ink" font-size="13">Member 2</text><text x="241.5" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-muted" font-size="12">BFD per member</text><line x1="308" y1="65" x2="330" y2="65" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="330" y="30" width="133" height="70" rx="10" fill="#ddd6fe"/><text x="396.5" y="58" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-ink" font-size="13">Bundle</text><text x="396.5" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-muted" font-size="12">logical IF par BFD</text><line x1="463" y1="65" x2="485" y2="65" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/><rect x="485" y="30" width="133" height="70" rx="10" fill="#fde68a"/><text x="551.5" y="58" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-ink" font-size="13">IGP</text><text x="551.5" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-muted" font-size="12">ek adjacency</text><text x="320" y="128" text-anchor="middle" class="svg-accent" font-size="12">Per-member BFD kharab member dhoondta hai; logical BFD kharab bundle path dhoondta hai</text></svg><p class="cap">Bundle ke liye do BFD options: har member link par ek session, ya logical interface par ek session.</p></div>
<h3>Bundle par BFD</h3>
<p>Agar aap Layer 3 bundle interface par BFD chalate ho to session hashing se chuna gaya ek member link path ki tarah use karta hai. Kisi doosre member par fault notice na ho aur us member par hash hua traffic LACP ya physical layer use hatane tak kho jaata hai. Isliye Cisco IOS XR do models deta hai:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>BFD over Bundle (BoB).</b> IETF-standard approach (RFC 7130, micro-BFD): <b>har member link par ek</b> BFD session. Member ka session fail ho to woh member bundle se nikal jaata hai aur bundle baaki members ke saath up rehta hai. Yeh kharab member jaldi dhoondta hai aur aisa member bhi pakadta hai jo physical layer par up hai lekin forward nahi kar raha.</li>
<li><b>BFD over Logical Bundle (BLB).</b> Bundle interface par ek hi BFD session, jo ise ek Layer 3 link maanta hai. Session down ho to poora bundle down declare hota hai. Simple hai, lekin ek samay mein sirf ek hashed path verify hota hai.</li>
</ul>
<pre class="code"><span class="c">! Reference configuration (simulator mein run nahi hoti)</span>
<span class="c">! IOS XR: per-member BFD (BoB)</span>
interface Bundle-Ether1
 bfd mode ietf
 bfd address-family ipv4 destination 198.51.100.2
 bfd address-family ipv4 fast-detect
 bfd address-family ipv4 minimum-interval 50
 bfd address-family ipv4 multiplier 3
 ipv4 address 198.51.100.1 255.255.255.252
 bundle minimum-links 2</pre>
<p>Exact options platform aur release par depend karte hain (jaise mode value aur echo use hota hai ya nahi), isliye block ko structure ka model samjho aur apne image ka command reference check karo. Exam aapse umeed karta hai ki aap dono models aur har ek chunne ki wajah jaante ho.</p>
<h3>Bundles aur IGP metric</h3>
<p>IS-IS aur OSPF har platform par bundle ki current bandwidth se uska cost automatically calculate nahi karte. IS-IS configured metric use karta hai, aur default sab interfaces par 10 hai. OSPF reference bandwidth aur interface bandwidth se cost nikalta hai. Chaar-member 40G bundle do members kho de to capacity aadhi ho jaati hai, lekin metric wahi rehta hai aur traffic aata rehta hai. Options: (1) manual metric jo degraded hone par bundle ko kam attractive banaye, aur <b>minimum-links</b> se threshold par down karna; (2) bundle bandwidth based metric, jaise OSPF <code>auto-cost</code> un platforms par jo bandwidth update karte hain; (3) TE-based placement. Hamesha tay karo ki aapka design kaunsa approach use karta hai aur failure test karo.</p>
<h3>ECMP versus LAG</h3>
<table class="cmp"><tr><th>Pehlu</th><th>LAG bundle</th><th>Alag L3 links par ECMP</th></tr><tr><td>Adjacencies</td><td>Ek IGP adjacency aur ek LDP session</td><td>Har link par ek</td></tr><tr><td>Member ka failure</td><td>Capacity girti hai, adjacency rehti hai</td><td>IGP converge hota hai, ECMP set chhota hota hai</td></tr><tr><td>Config effort</td><td>Bundle plus members</td><td>Har link par address</td></tr><tr><td>Load balancing</td><td>Members par per-flow hash (L2/L3/L4)</td><td>Next hop set par per-flow hash</td></tr><tr><td>Scale</td><td>Kam routes aur adjacencies</td><td>Zyada state, behtar failure handling</td></tr><tr><td>Typical SP use</td><td>P routers ke beech core links, 100G aggregates</td><td>Spine-leaf style aur kuch TE designs</td></tr></table>
<h3>Multichassis designs</h3>
<p>Customer ya access device aksar <b>do alag PE routers</b> se jaata hai taaki router failure use isolate na kare. Do methods important hain. <b>mLACP / MC-LAG</b> (multichassis LACP) do chassis ko ek LACP partner ki tarah present karta hai aur inter-chassis link (ICCP) par state synchronise karta hai. Kaam karta hai lekin careful synchronisation chahiye aur operational complexity hai. <b>EVPN multihoming with ESI</b> (Ethernet Segment Identifier) modern, standards-based replacement hai: dono PEs ek ESI aur usse nikla LACP system ID share karte hain, aur BGP EVPN routes designated forwarder coordinate karte hain. Dhyan do ki EVPN aage ke SP modules mein detail se aayega; abhi dono naam aur yeh jaano ki all-active multihoming traffic dono PEs par baantta hai.</p>
<div class="eg"><p><b>Worked example.</b> P1 aur P2 ke beech Bundle-Ether1 chaar 100G members (400G) ke saath chalta hai. Aap minimum-links 2, 50 ms guna 3 par per-member BFD aur IS-IS metric 10 set karte ho. Transponder fault chupke se ek member ka traffic girata hai jabki uske optics up rehte hain. Fast rate par LACP 3 second leta. Us member ka BoB session 150 ms mein fail hota hai, member bundle se nikal jaata hai, aur hash flows ko teen bache members par redistribute karta hai. Bundle 300G ke saath up rehta hai.</p></div>
<div class="warn"><p><b>Common mistake.</b> Ek logical-bundle BFD session lagakar maan lena ki har member verify ho gaya. Session ek hashed path follow karta hai. Doosre member ka silent fault dikhta nahi. Saath hi bade bundle ke har member par aggressive BFD lagane se pehle platform scale check karo: har session resources leta hai, aur line card par sessions ki ginti limited hoti hai.</p></div>
<div class="exam"><p><b>Exam trap.</b> BFD over Bundle (per member, RFC 7130 micro-BFD) fail hue <i>member</i> ko hata deta hai; BFD over Logical Bundle poore bundle ko down kar deta hai. LACP member failure ko BFD ke muqable dheere detect karta hai (fast mein 3 second, slow mein 90 second). Bundle har platform par IS-IS metric automatically nahi badalta.</p></div>
<div class="scn"><h4>Silent member</h4><p>Do P routers ke beech 4 x 100G bundle mein koi alarm nahi tha, phir bhi kuch customers ko lagbhag 25 percent packet loss dikha. Optical transport mein ek transponder aise fail hua tha ki router port lit raha. LACP ne member nahi hataya kyunki partner doosre internal path se LACPDUs bhejta raha. Incident ke baad team logical-bundle BFD se per-member BFD par gayi, aur agle transponder fault mein kharab member 150 ms mein hat gaya.</p><p><b>Lesson:</b> har member verify karo, sirf ek hashed path nahi.</p></div>
<div class="iv"><p class="q">"IOS XR par BFD over Bundle aur BFD over Logical Bundle ki tulna karo."</p><p>BoB har member par ek BFD session chalata hai (micro-BFD, RFC 7130). Failure sirf us member ko hatata hai aur bundle up rehta hai, isliye silent member faults pakadta hai lekin zyada sessions leta hai. BLB bundle par Layer 3 interface ki tarah ek session chalata hai aur failure par poora bundle down karta hai; simple hai lekin ek hashed path verify karta hai. Jodo ki minimum-links tay karta hai ki degraded bundle kab down hona chahiye.</p></div>
<div class="kt"><p><b>Key takeaways</b></p><ul>
<li>BoB: har member link par BFD, kharab member ko hataata hai (micro-BFD, RFC 7130).</li>
<li>BLB: logical bundle par ek BFD session, poora bundle down karta hai.</li>
<li>IGP metric har platform par bundle bandwidth ko automatically follow nahi karta; minimum-links aur soch-samajh kar metric plan use karo.</li>
<li>ECMP har link par ek adjacency deta hai; LAG har bundle par ek adjacency.</li>
<li>MC-LAG (ICCP ke saath mLACP) aur EVPN ESI multihoming device ko do PEs se jodte hain.</li>
</ul></div>
`}};export{e as default};